0s"Agaricales "A “Mushrooms” – fleshy and not perennial, with gills, on many substrates011313702
0s"Boletaceae" "B “Mushrooms” – fleshy and not perennial, with pores, on many substrates0114138054
0s"Hydnaceae"C “Mushrooms” – typically fleshy and not perennial, with teeth, on many substrates0115139062
0s"Chantarelles"D “Mushrooms” – fleshy and funnel shaped, smooth or with ridges, the chantarelles01161310071
0s"Polypores"E Bracket fungi – woody and persistent, on trees and dead wood01171311085
0s"Puffballs, earthstars, and bird's nest fungi"F Puffballs, earth stars, bird's nest fungi – on soil, leaves or detritus, often tough011813120101
0s"Ascomycetes"G Ascomycetes – fleshy and typically delicate011913130128
0s"Coral fungi"H Coral fungi –branched or clubbed, often tough0111013140147
0s"Jelly fungi"I Jelly fungi –moist and gelatinous, becoming dry and tough, on wood0111113150154
0s"Stinkhorns"J Stinkhorns –soft club-shaped, and smelly, on soil0111213160159
0s"Club shaped fungi"K Club shaped fungi – stinkhorns, dead mens’ fingers, earth tongues, etc.0111313170165
0s"Crust fungi"L Crust fungi – dry, smooth or with pores or teeth, on wood0111413180166
0s"subterranean fungi."M Subterranean fungi0111513190175
0s"Slime molds"N Slime molds0111613200177
0_(1A) Gilled mushrooms – the Agaricales – these include classic "mushrooms" that have a cap and a stem, with the spores produced on thin "leaves" called gills, on the underside _(exception, Schizophyllum) of the cap. Some agarics, particularly those growing on wood, have an eccentric stalk, or no stalk and the cap appears to grow directly from the substrate. See the pictorial glossary for major variations in shape and the terms glossary for descriptive words. Spore colour is important for the Agaricales, and is not always related to gill colour – some coloured gills have white spores and vice versa – make a spore print! 0231714210Gilled mushrooms – the Agaricales0
0a _(1A) White or light coloured spores02318142203
0b _(1A) Pink spores02319142307
0c _(1A) Dark coloured spores _(cinnamon, purple-brown, yellow-brown, grey, nearly black)02320142408
0a _(2a) Often on ground, but many substrates031821152504
0b _(2a) Often on wood, but identify by elimination Tricholomataceae,0318221526012
0a _(3a) Gills free from stalk042123162705
0b _(3a) Gills attached to stalk042124162806
0a _(4a) Volva _(cup) present at base of stem, which may be fragile and also be left as warts on the cap; ring present or absent Amanitaceae,0523251729034
0b _(4a) No volva of any kind, but base of stem may be bulbous; may have scales on cap, but never warts; ring usually present and often breaking loose Lepiotaceae,0523261730035
0a _(4b) Gills thick and fleshy, waxy when rubbed; cap often brightly coloured; always on ground; ring usually absent Hygrophoraceae,0624271831036
0b _(4b) On ground, usually near but not on wood; gills free or attached; cap often colourful and peels easily; sturdy, brittle stipe Russulaceae,0624281832037
0c _(4b) Many habitats but often on wood; latex absent Tricholomataceae,0624291833012
0a _(2b) Gills free from stem; usually on wood; volva present or absent Volvariaceae,0719301934038
0b _(2b) Gills attached to stem; usually on ground; volva absent Rhodophyllaceae,0719311935039
0a _(2c) Gills free; cap easily separable from stem Agaricaceae,0820322036040
0b _(2c) On ground, usually near but not on wood; gills free or attached; cap often colourful and peels easily; sturdy, brittle stipe £Russulaceae,0820332037037
0c _(2c) Gills attached082034203809
0a _(8c) Gills notably extending down stem _(decurrent), or no stem0934352139010
0b _(8c) Gills attached broadly _(adnate) or narrowly _(adnexed)0934362140011
0a _(14b) Gill edges serrate _(sawtooth) 0183653730410Lentinus0
0b _(14b) Gill edges smooth 0183653830420Xeromphalina0
0c _(14b) Gill edges smooth; cap covered with stiff hairs 0183653930430Crinipellis0
0a _(13b) Gill edges serrate or hairy019338403144020
0b _(13b) Gill edges smooth019338413145021
0a _(19a) Stem always absent; gills facing upwards, and split down middle 020404232460Schizophyllum0
0b _(19a) Stem absent or eccentric; sawtooth gill edges 020404332470Lentinellus0
0a _(19b) Cap flesh-coloured to brick red with reticulate ridges 021414433480Rhodotus0
0b _(19b) Cap smooth; stem short or absent; flesh soft or tough 021414533490Pleurotus0
0a _(12b) Stem with a ring022310463450023
0b _(12b) Stem lacking a ring022310473451024
0a _(22a) Ring always single; veil and cap appearing mealy or powdery 023464835520Cystoderma0
0b _(22a) Ring single or double; veil and cap not appearing mealy 023464935530Armillaria and Catathelasma0
0a _(22b) Fruiting body revives in water; stem 1-4 mm wide and pliant02447503654025
0b _(22b) Fruiting body does not revive in water –or– stem usually more than 4mm wide or fleshy or brittle02447513655027
0a _(24a) Cap covered with long stiff hairs 025505237560Crinipellis0
0b _(24a) Cap smooth or felty02550533757026
0a _(25b) Cap surface composed of round cells; small delicate mushrooms often in troops; stem yellow brown to black 026535438580Marasmius0
0b _(25b) Cap cuticle composed of hyphae; small delicate mushrooms often in troops; spores turn blue in iodine 026535538590Xeromphalina0
0a _(24b) Parasitic/saprotrophic on decaying mushrooms 027515639600Asterophora0
0b _(24b) Not parasitic/saprotrophic on decaying mushrooms02751573961028
0a _(27b) Gills adnexed to decurrent _(may be subtle) 028575840620Clitocybe/Omphalina/Omphalotus0
0b _(27b) Gills adnate to adnexed but never decurrent02857594063029
0a _(28b) Cap margin inrolled at first; stem brittle, sometimes stuffed; does not revive; in humus, on leaves or rotten wood; often in fairy rings 029596041640Collybia, Flammulina/Baeospora0
0b _(28b) Cap margin straight or, if inrolled then stem is fleshy02959614165030
0a _(29b) Relatively small and fragile; stem brittle and 1-4mm wide, equal, lacking a ring; gills broadly attached to notched, seldom decurrent; gills may be coloured, but spore print white 030616242660Mycena0
0b _(29b) Stem thicker and fleshy to pliant03061634267031
0a _(30b) Gills fleshy; gills or cap pink or violet 031636443680Laccaria, or Mycena pura0
0b _(30b) Gills not fleshy, and usually no part pink or violet03163654369032
0a _(31b) Gills bruising black, stem appears water-soaked 032656644700Lyophyllum0
0b _(31b) Gills not bruising black03265674471033
0a _(32b) Cap 6-45 cm broad and edge may be fluted; surface of gills and stem chalky white 033676845720Leucopaxillus0
0b _(32b) Stem long and thin for size of cap; cap convex and knobbed; gills notched 033676945730Melanoleuca0
0c _(32b) Stem thick, fleshy, robust; cap convex and sometimes with a depressed centre; gills broadly attached or notched 033677045740Tricholoma0
0_(5a) Amanitaceae Many in this family are poisonous, often deadly, so should be avoided as potential menu items. Common features in the field include 1) white spores, 2) development from an "egg" which when cut open has stem, cap and gills _(unlike young puffballs), 3) a universal veil which either splits creating the cup _(volva) at the base of the stem and may also leave warts on the cap, 4) a partial veil that leaves a ring _(annulus) on the stem. Two genera. 034257146750Amanitaceae0
0a _(5a) Volva and/or universal veil slimy and glutinous 034257246760Limacella0
0b _(5a) Volva and/or universal veil fibrous or filmy or hairy 034257346770Amanita0
0_(5b) Lepiotaceae This group is relatively closely related to Amanitaceae since both groups have free gills and white spores _(Chlorophyllum has green spores), but the Lepiotaceae do not have a volva even if their stem is bulbous. The cap surface is often scaly, but these are not due to remnants of the partial veil, which often forms a ring. This ring can become entirely free and slide along the stem. Many Lepiota species fruit in grass, often in fairy rings, while others appear to be mycorrhizal with conifers 035267447780Lepiotaceae0
0a _(5b) Spore print and mature gills green, in grass 035267547790Chlorophyllum0
0b _(5b) Spore print and mature gills white, various habitats 035267647800Lepiota0
0_(6a) Hygrophoraceae This is a typically brightly coloured group _(nevertheless, the spores are white) that always grows in the ground or on duff, and not on logs or other wood, or on dung, or parasitically. When the gills are rubbed together very hard they feel waxy, and leave a waxy layer on the fingers. They do not produce latex. There is one genus, 036277748810Hygrophorus.0
0_(6b) Russulaceae There are two genera in this family, both with brittle caps whose often brightly coloured cuticle peels off easily, and brittle stems that never have a ring. Both genera have white, pale brown, or yellow spores that turn blue in iodine. All species in this family grow on soil, and are typically thought to be mycorrhizal. A679-707; Ar63-103; M 62-86; 037337849820Russulaceae0
0a _(6b) Produces a milky latex when cut 037337949830Lactarius0
0b _(6b) Does not produce latex when cut 037338049840Russula0
0_(7a) Volvariaceae One of two pink-spored families of gilled fungi. The most conspicuous characteristic of Volvariaceae is the free gills. There are two genera both of which are common _(but not exclusive) on wood 038308150850Volvariaceae0
0a _(7a) Volva present at base of stem, on wood or other substrates 038308250860Volvariella0
0b _(7a) Volva absent, always on wood 038308350870Pluteus0
0_(7b) Rhodophyllaceae The most conspicuous feature of this pink-spored family is the attached gills. Species of this family that have central stalks are always on ground and never on wood. 039318451880Rhodophyllaceae0
0a _(7b) Gills long and decurrent 039318551890Clitopilus0
0b _(7b) Gills short and decurrent or notched, often with a conic cap or a knob on a convex cap 039318651900Entoloma0
0_(8a) Agaricaceae Aroira and Miller recognize one genus, Agaricus, but Lincoff _(Audubon guide) includes additional genera including Lepiota. Agaricus contains a large number of edible species mushrooms form which the commercial A. bisporus was bred. Typically Agaricus are edible and choice, with the exception of A. xanthodermus and allies, which are poisonous. A. xanthodermus bruises yellow, and all Agaricus species that bruise yellowish should be avoided. The other caution is to ensure the spores are dark, since the fruiting bodies can superficially resemble Amanita. 040328752910Agaricaceae0
0_(10a) Paxillaceae. Miller recognizes one genus, Paxillus, while Audubon includes three. Paxillus has clay to yellow brown spores and decurrent gills; two species grow on wood, and one on soil 0412508853920Paxillaceae.0
0_(10b) Gomphidiaceae Most notably, this family has thick decurrent gills and smoky-grey to black spores. There are two genera. A650-4; Ar481-7; Bo16; M164-9; 0422518954930Gomphidiaceae0
0a _(10b) Cap cuticle gelatinous; white flesh in cap unreactive to iodine 0422519054940Gomphidius0
0b _(10b) Cap dry to viscid or tacky; coloured flesh in cap turns blue in iodine 0422519154950Chroogomphus0
0_(11a) Coprinaceae. This family has black to purple-brown spores that are borne on attached or notched gills, which may deliquesce into inky slime. The cap is often conical with a dry to viscid cuticle, and the stem is usually relatively thin and fragile. All are saprophytes on dung, humus, grassy areas. Several are poisonous, and even the edible ones should not be eaten with alcohol. 0432759255960Coprinaceae0
0a _(11a) Gills white on young fruiting bodies, then black and inky with age, and/or cap with umbrella-like striations; on wood, humus, dung, grass 0432759355970Coprinus0
0b _(11a) Cap narrowly conic, brittle; with a thin rigid stem; single to numerous on dung or in grass, but not cespitose 0432759455980Panaeolus0
0c _(11a) Campanulate to convex cap, usually on humus or wood, often numerous or cespitose; many species 0432759555990Psathyrella0
0_(11b) Bolbitiaceae. This family is closely related to the Coprinaceae; the fruiting bodies are superficially similar, but they have lighter coloured spores. 04427696561000Bolbitiaceae0
0a _(11b) Stem tall and 1.2-3mm wide; cap narrowly conical and delicate0442769756101045
0b _(11b) Stem thicker than 3mm or if thin then cap is convex or yellow0442769856102046
0a _(44a) Cap white to pale cinnamon; gills nearly free 0459799571030Conocybe lactea0
0b _(44a) Cap yellow- to red-brown; gills nearly free 04597100571040Conocybe tenera0
0a _(44b) Cap yellow, bell shaped, margin striate; on cow dung in grass 04698101581050Bolbitius vitellinus0
0b _(44b) Cap convex, cream coloured to tan to brown, not striate 04698102581060Agrocybe0
0_(11c) Cortinariaceae. This is a large family with brown spores and entire gills that are always attached to the stem. Many species have the characteristic cobwebby/fibrous veil _(called a cortina) that collapses to leave a thready ring on the stem. None are found on dung, but many are saprophytic on wood, humus or soil. Many others are mycorrhizal with hardwoods or conifers. A556-8, 611-23, 712-8; Ar396-466; M202-225 047277103591070Cortinariaceae0
0a _(11c) On wood, sometimes buried wood04727710459108048
0b _(11c) On ground, and not from buried wood04727710559109050
0a _(47a) Stem absent 048104106601100Crepidotus0
0b _(47a) Stem present, central or eccentric04810410760111049
0a _(48b) Fruiting body and spore print bright rusty orange to brown; cap hairless when young; stem central; from wood 049107108611120Gymnopilus0
0b _(48b) Spore print dull brown; cap and/or stem typically scaly or knobby; cespitose 049107109611130Pholiota0
0a _(47b) Veil membranous, cap orange, nearly flat, and radially wrinkled, never in cespitose clusters 050105110621140Rozites caperata0
0b _(47b) Veil, if present, hairy or cobwebby05010511162115051
0a _(50b) Stem thin, brittle, fragile, with a ring; cap thin, fragile, often striate, usually hairless, conic to convex and sometimes with a shallow depression 051111112631160Galerina0
0b _(50b) Stem fleshy to pliant, not brittle or fragile; cap not fragile, variously shaped, seldom striate05111111363117052
0a _(51b) Cap dry, conic to bell shaped, often hairy; gill edge with clavate cystidia 052113114641180Inocybe0
0b _(51b) Cap viscid, usually convex; veil present or absent; stem fleshy and dry; cylindric cystidia on gill edge 052113115641190Hebeloma0
0c _(51b) Cap dry or viscid; if viscid then no cystidia on gill edge; cobwebby veil 052113116641200Cortinarius0
0_(11d) Strophariaceae. This family has purple-brown spores, a central stem, and attached gills. The Agaricaceae have free gills. Continariaceae have red- to yellow-brown spores and a hairy annulus that may be a few persistent fibrils on the stem. 053278117651210Strophariaceae0
0a _(11d) Persistent superior ring _(except for S. aeruginosa which has almost no ring, but a viscous yellow to bright green cap) 053278118651220Stropharia0
0b _(11d) Thin stem, conic cap, moist-tacky-viscid; with to without ring; never cespitose; on dung, grass or humus 053278119651230Psilocybe0
0c _(11d) Convex cap, sometimes with low knob, occasionally with thickened stem; on wood 053278120651240Naematatoloma0
0_(1B). Mushrooms with pores, fleshy and not perennial – family Boletaceae. – Superficial resemblance to gilled mushrooms, but with spore-bearing surface _(hymenium) arranged on fine or coarse pores, sometimes in loose radial rows _(boletoid arrangement). 0544121661250Boletaceae0
0a _(1B) Cap and stalk conspicuously covered with coarse, dry black/brown scales; spores umber brown to blackish brown. Pores angular. Wooly ring_(s) on the stalk. Flesh bruising reddish, then black. 0544122661260Strobilomyces0
0b _(1B) If cap and stem have scales, then spores not brown/black054412366127055
0a _(54b) Tubes not consistently vertical, rather contorted and may be closed. Mostly in the western regions 055123124671280Gastroboletus turbinatus0
0b _(54b) Tubes straight and open05512312567129056
0a _(55b) Tubes radially arranged in rows, with arrangement most noticeable near the stalk05612512668130057
0b _(55b) Tubes not radial; mouths most often round to angular, cap dry05612512768131058
0c _(55b) Tubes not radial; mouths most often round to angular, cap viscid 056125128681320Suillus0
0a _(56a) Mouths of tubes uneven; yellow; strong radial pattern resembling gills with cross-gills; 2 5mm broad, and shallow; cap brown and dry 057126129691330Gyrodon merulioides0
0b _(56a) Mouths of tubes even; tubes long05712613069134059
0a _(56b) Mouths of tubes white, then pink with age; spore print pink; bruises brown; stem with netlike ridges; taste extremely bitter 058127131701350Tylopilus0
0b _(56b) Mouths of tubes may be white, but not turning pink with age; not bruising brown; stalk may or may not have netlike ridges; taste mild to bitter05812713270136060
0a _(57b) Cap dry to moist; pores have strong radial arrangement; spores wine red to reddish brown 059130133711370Fuscoboletinus0
0b _(457b) Cap usually viscid to slimy; if dry then pores radially arranged; spores cinnamon or olive brown or brown 059130134711380Suillus0
0a _(58b) Stalk with conspicuous dark brown to black hairy tufts at maturity 060132135721390Leccinum0
0b _(58b) Stalk never with dark brown to black hairy tufts06013213672140061
0a _(60b) Spore print yellow; spores white to pale yellow; stalk not having netlike ridges 061136137731410Gyroporus0
0b _(60b) Cap dry; tubes fine to coarse but not radially arranged; spore print olive brown to yellow brown 061136138731420Boletus0
0_(1C) Fungi with spore-bearing teeth – Hydnaceae - sometimes with a appearance similar to gilled mushrooms, or like a bracket fungus – a variable group. 0625139741430Hydnaceae0
0a _(1C) Cap absent; fruit body covered with long, white, hanging teeth062514074144063
