0c _(116a) Stalk short, spore sac naked 01172662701292740Calostoma ravenelii0
0a _(116b) Stalk >10 cm long, in desert regions01182672711302750119
0b _(116b) Stalk 1.5-6 cm long, tough to woody, widely distributed 01182672721302760Tulostoma simulans0
0a _(118a) Stalk 20-40 cm long, 6-15 mm wide, volva simple 01192712731312770Battarrea phalloides0
0b _(118a) Stalk 10-45 cm long, 20-40 mm wide, volva with several layers 01192712741312780Battarrea laciniata0
0a _(9b) Caps typically conic to bell shaped; often turning inky but when young, cap is never slimy; saprotrophs, they are often found growing on dung or manured grass or wood Coprinaceae,0113627523279043
0b _(9b) Similar to Coprinaceae, but never turning inky; fragile mushrooms that typically grow in grass with long thin stipes Bolbitiaceae,0113627623280044
0c _(9b) With a fibrous partial veil that often leaves a cobwebby ring on the stipe; never on dung; typically saprophytic on wood, humus or soil, or mycorrhizal Cortinariaceae,0113627723281047
0d _(9b) Sturdy fruiting bodies with a convex viscous cap; persistent annulus that is never cobwebby; usually on grass, mulch or dung Strophariaceae,0113627823282053
0_(3b) Tricholomataceae Any white-spored fungus that grows on wood is a member of this family, but in addition they are also found in diverse habitats and can be saprotrophic or mycorrhizal. A731-810; Ar129-237; L54-68; M87-158; Mc133; S64-91, 239 01229279242830Tricholomataceae0
0_(101b) False puffballs. Outwardly similar to a puffball, but spore mass never powdery. 01202312801322840False puffballs0
0a _(101b) Fruit body 1-5 cm wide, cross section shows only tiny chambers; rounded rubbery white to yellow peridium is stained red 01202312811322850Rhizopogon rubescens0
0b _(101b) Gleba with branched, buff-coloured flesh, or fruiting body on a stalk01202312821322860121
0a _(120b) Fruiting body pear shaped, greenish yellow, dry; gleba has branched, sterile buff flesh 01212822831332870Truncocolumella citrina0
0b _(120b) Fruiting body with an obvious stem01212822841332880122
0a _(121b) Fruiting body stalk 15-30 cm long and narrow cylindric cap 6-10cm tall; in desert 01222842851342890Podaxis pistillaris0
0b _(121b) Stalk no more than 25 mm long; on conifer wood near snowbanks; cap yellow brown and viscid, rounded 01222842861342900Nivatogastrium nubigenum0
0c _(121b) On earth, especially in flowerbeds and nursries; cap dingy white, hairy 01222842871342910Endoptychum agaricoides0
0_(101b) Earthstars are a distinctive group of fungi related to puffballs, that have an outer and an inner wall. The outer wall splits at maturity _(forming rays) to expose the inner wall, but may close up under some conditions. The inner wall surrounds the spore sac _(gleba) and may develop a pore _(ostiole) at maturity. Ar699-706; M295-8 01232262881352920Earthstars0
0a _(101b) Rays open when wet, close around the spore sac when dry. Spore sac hairy 01232262891352930Astraeus hygrometricus0
0b _(101b) Rays remain open, inner spore sac smooth01232262901352940124
0a _(123b) Spore sac with many ostioles 01242902911362950Myriostoma coliforme0
0b _(123b) Spore sac with one hole _(ostiole) at the top01242902921362960125
0a _(124b) Inner spore sac on a short stalk 01252922931372970Geastrum coronatum0
0b _(124b) Inner spore sac not on a stalk01252922941372980126
0a _(125b) Spore sac in an 'bowl' when the rays open; inner surface of the bowl cracked 01262942951382990Geastrum triplex0
0b _(125b) Spore sac exposed but not on a stalk; inner surface of the ray smooth 01262942961383000Geastrum saccatum0
0_(101c) Bird's nest fungi are a distinctive group with tiny _(4-15 mm wide) fruiting bodies most of which remarkably resemble their common name; commonly on sticks or woody debris. There are several genera, including the most common ones described here. Ar778 81 01272272971393010Bird's nest fungi0
0a _(101c) Fruiting body 10-15 mm wide and high; cinnamon to grey brown; membranous covering over the top later revealing a striped inner wall; "eggs" _(peridioles) white 01272272981393020Cyathus striatus0
0b _(101c) As for Cyathus, but inner walls not striate; peridioles nearly white 01272272991393030Crucibilum vulgare0
0c _(101c) Fruiting body 3-10 mm wide and 5-20mm high, with a flaring mouth. "Eggs" grey to light brown, embedded in a gel. 01272273001393040Nidula candida0
0d _(101c) Fruiting body 1.5 mm and spherical, yellow orange to whitish at maturity. The single "egg" is shot off by a ballistic mechanism given sufficient moisture 01272273011393050Sphaerobolus stellatus0
0_(1G) Ascomycetes is a large group of typically small fungi, seldom more than 5 cm in the largest dimension, and often microscopic. Fruiting bodies are typically delicate, but may also be tough or leathery, particularly when dry. Ar782-887; M324-46 012893021403060Ascomycetes0
0a _(1G) Resembling a mushroom with a 'stem' and 'cap', both of which may have an uneven _(pitted) surface, but the stem hollow and the cap never having gills, pores or teeth012893031403070129
0b _(1G) Resembling a cup012893041403080139
0c _(1G) Resembling a tongue_(s) or finger_(s) growing from soil or litter012893051403090145
0_(128a) Morels and false morels Morels are delicious and highly prized edible fungi, but the superficially similar false morels are generally poisonous. Typically morels resemble pine cones on stalks, fruit in springtime particularly in a forest the years first following a fire; false morels fruit spring and autumn. Ar784-816; M324-335 01293033061413100Morels and false morels0
0a _(128a) Head and stem continuous, hollow; head ridged or pitted _(true morel)01293033071413110130
0b _(114a) Head or cap attached at the top of the stalk, not hollow; head wrinkled or smooth or saddled shaped, but not ridged or pitted except on the stem _(false morel)01293033081413120133
0a _(3b) Only on wood, stem central, eccentric, or lacking0122930924313013
0b _(3b) On ground, needles, humus, dung, but rare on wood; stem usually central0122931024314022
